<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 5 – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Move Around</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures and drawings to express movement actions (e.g., jumping up and down). Can clarify or ask for repetition when unsure about movement instructions (e.g., 'Repeat, please.'). Can act out scenes from picture books or songs involving dance movements (e.g., making a dance movement seen in the video of a song).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game focusing on movement verbs and prepositions previously learned. (e.g., "Simon says: Jump up!", "Simon says: Step to the side!", "Simon says: Turn around!"). The teacher introduces and models asking for clarification: "Repeat, please." The class practices chanting the /t/ sound in 'two' and the /d/ sound in 'dance' while performing movements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to ask for repetition and how to use simple gestures to show a movement command. For example, the teacher says "Move to the front!" and gestures forward. Then the teacher pretends not to understand a student's (simulated) instruction and models, "Repeat, please?" while cupping an ear. The teacher then models demonstrating the instruction using gestures for the whole class to see.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to self gesturing "jump" and asks: "What am I doing?" (Expected: "Jump!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher gives an instruction, then pauses and asks: "If I don't understand, what can I say?" (Expected: "Repeat, please.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple video of children performing a basic dance routine with clear, distinct steps (e.g., "One step in, two steps out, turn around."). The video should visually demonstrate the sequential commands and positional prepositions. Focus on 'J' in 'jump' and introduce letters S, T, U, V by pointing to words in a simple chart or flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Acting out dance commands: Follow simple movement instructions in dance, like 'step forward' and 'clap'. Reciting action rhymes: Participate in action rhymes or songs, such as 'Head, Shoulders, Knees, and Toes'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students watch the video. Teacher asks: "Do they jump?" (Students nod/shake heads or say "Yes!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher pauses the video at a specific action (e.g., "two steps to the side") and asks: "How many steps?" (Students hold up two fingers or say "Two!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Are they dancing fast or slow?" (Students say "Fast!" or "Slow!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will use gestures and simple directive phrases (e.g., 'Move to the front.') and sequential commands ('First, take one step...', 'Next, take two steps...') with numbers and prepositions (e.g., 'next to', 'over') to mediate understanding of dance movements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Action-to-Drawing Mediation:</b> The teacher performs a simple dance movement (e.g., "Jump up and down"). Students first imitate the action. Then, the teacher gives each student a small whiteboard or paper and asks them to draw what they just did. The teacher then collects drawings and asks students to explain their drawing with a gesture and a simple word (e.g., points to drawing, jumps, says "Jump!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Gesture-to-Word Mediation (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher provides picture cards showing different movements (e.g., "spin around," "hop on one foot," "step two steps forward"). Student A picks a card, performs the action silently. Student B tries to say the command (e.g., "Spin around!") and Student A confirms with a nod. If Student B is unsure, they practice saying "Repeat, please?" and Student A repeats the gesture. Encourage polite expressions like 'go ahead' for turn-taking.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Explaining a Dance Sequence (Group Circle):</b> The teacher demonstrates a short, two-step dance sequence (e.g., "First, two steps to the side. Next, turn around!"). Students watch and then one student explains it to the class using gestures and the sequential commands. Other students perform. The teacher encourages students to help each other understand by re-explaining with different gestures or asking "Repeat, please?" if a peer is unclear. Focus on 'voice' clarity.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 children) to prepare a simple "Dance Sequence Video." Each group will create a 2-3 step dance routine using learned vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "First, one step in. Next, two steps out. Then, jump up!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Collaborative video recording. One student in each group will be the "explainer" who uses gestures and simple English commands to tell the others what to do for each step of their routine. The other students will follow the instructions. The teacher will record short clips of each group's explanation and performance using a tablet or phone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups quickly decide on their 2-3 step dance sequence.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student (the "explainer") practices explaining the sequence using gestures and sequential commands. The others practice following.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When ready, the teacher records: the "explainer" shows and says the steps (e.g., "First, move around! Next, hop, hop, hop!"), and the group performs the dance.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups rotate explainers, allowing multiple students to mediate. Encourage polite phrases like 'after you' for taking turns.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Dance Sequence Video" recording and a simple peer assessment during a short sharing session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their dance sequence and explain it to their peers/camera using gestures and simple commands. Other students respond by performing the action. The teacher observes how effectively students mediate the instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Uses clear gestures and simple words/phrases to convey movement (e.g., 'jump,' 'step,' 'turn').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary/Grammar:</b> Incorporates relevant verbs (e.g., 'move', 'dance', 'hop'), numbers (e.g., 'one', 'two'), and simple sequential/positional commands (e.g., 'First, ...', 'Next, ...', 'in', 'out', 'next to').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation and Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in group tasks, follows instructions, and takes turns. Attempts to clarify or ask for repetition if unsure ('Repeat, please.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Demonstrates intelligible pronunciation of key words, especially /t/ and /d/.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you help your friends understand the dance?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you understand your friends' dance moves?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or hard to explain with your hands?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students use gestures and simple words to explain dance movements? Was the "Repeat, please" strategy effectively used?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed to further develop their mediation skills, especially in linking actions to drawings or words? (e.g., more direct modeling of drawing dance steps).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Move Around" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly reviewing movement verbs but within a new context and with new vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>